{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Symptom\n\nRunning plan review through the MCP server (`review_plan_start` / `review_plan`) can\nreview a **stale copy of the ticket description** instead of the current one. After\nediting a ticket's description to address blocking findings, a fresh MCP review returned\n**byte-identical findings** -- including findings whose text had already been removed\nfrom the plan -- and recorded a `material_parts.description` fingerprint matching the\npre-edit description rather than the edited one.\n\nRunning `rebar review-plan <id>` from a local worktree immediately afterwards, with no\nother change, read the current description and produced different (correct) findings.\n\n## Why this is serious\n\nPlan review is a **gate that mints a signed attestation**, and the attestation's whole\nvalue is that it binds a verdict to specific pinned material. If the material the MCP\npath pins is not the material in the store, then:\n\n- A plan can be BLOCKED for findings the author has already fixed, with no way to clear\n  them through the MCP path -- the author edits, re-reviews, and gets the same verdict.\n- More dangerously, the inverse: a plan can be PASSED and certified against a description\n  that is no longer what the ticket says.\n\n`AGENTS.md` instructs agents to route tracker operations, explicitly including\n`review-plan`, through the MCP server when one is configured. So the documented default\npath is the affected one, and the working path (local CLI) is documented as the fallback.\n\n## Evidence\n\nObserved in session `cd818479-c2e0-4a67-a07f-2d230e321466` on more than one ticket. The\ntell is the `material_fingerprint` / `material_parts.description` in the review result:\ncompare it against a fingerprint computed from the description that `show_ticket` returns\nat the same moment. When they disagree, the review ran on stale material.\n\n## Root cause (confirmed 2026-10-18, origin/main 3beb7e4ad23)\n\nTwo independent windows exist. Only W1 is fixed here; W2 is split out.\n\n**W1 — dedup attaches a post-edit start to a pre-edit run (fixed here).** The MCP singleflight\nkey (`src/rebar/_mcp_inflight.py` `compute_key`) hashes gate, canonical ticket id, code basis\nSHA, variant, and readonly flag. Plan material is not in it. Both plan-review call sites in\n`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py` (`review_plan_start` ~line 258, sync `review_plan` ~line 445) pass\n`variant=f\"source={source or 'attested'}\"`. So while a review of ticket T is in flight, an\nedit to T followed by a new start returns the SAME job, whose material was snapshotted at its\nown start. The caller then polls byte-identical findings pinned to the pre-edit description —\nthe reported symptom. Window: the remaining lifetime of the in-flight run (a review takes 15\nto 20 minutes); the key is purged when the run completes, so a start after completion is fresh.\n\nReproduced in-process with no LLM: `begin_gate_job` on a fresh ticket, `edit_ticket` the\ndescription, `begin_gate_job` again -> `is_new=False`, same `job_id`, while `show_ticket`\nalready returns the edited text.\n\nSigning is NOT the hole for a same-store edit: `generation.sign_manifest` re-collects the\nmaterial under the store lock and raises `PlanReviewGenerationChanged` when the ticket's own\nmaterial moved, so the attached run cannot sign over the edit. The defect is that the edited\nplan never gets reviewed through the start the author issued after editing.\n\n**W2 — cross-clone replication lag (out of scope; follow-up ticket).** The server reads its\nown tickets clone; an edit made in another clone reaches it only through\n`reads.ensure_fresh`, a `git fetch` throttled to once per 60 s. A review started within 60 s\nof the server's previous fetch reads the pre-edit copy. Fixing this changes the shared\nread-freshness/locking path and is tracked separately.\n\nForensic check of this repo's tracker: 4210 REVIEW_RESULT events compared against the\ndescription current at each review's timestamp. No result pins a description older than the\none current when its run started; the one review whose pin predates an edit (326f-595a,\n2026-08-27 17:11 PDT, BLOCK, unsigned) had the edit land during the run.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Add `_plan_review_variant(ticket_id, source)` in `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py` returning\n   `f\"source={source or 'attested'};material={fp}\"`, where `fp` is\n   `rebar.llm.plan_review.attest.current_material_fingerprint(ticket_id)` (a light read, no\n   LLM; returns None on a read failure, which keeps the old key behaviour for that call).\n2. Use it at both plan-review call sites. `_mcp_inflight.py` is unchanged: material enters\n   through the existing `variant` dimension.\n3. `verify_completion` dedup is unchanged here (the ticket is about plan review).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] Regression test in `tests/unit/test_mcp_llm_dedup.py`: on a real temp store, a blocked\n      `review_plan_start` run is in flight, the description is edited, and a second\n      `review_plan_start` returns a NEW job whose gate call happens after the edit. The test\n      fails on origin/main (same job id returned) and passes with the fix.\n- [x] Same check through the sync `review_plan` tool: after an edit, a concurrent call runs\n      the gate a second time instead of sharing the first result.\n- [x] Unchanged material still de-duplicates: the existing\n      `test_two_concurrent_calls_invoke_the_gate_exactly_once` cases pass unchanged.\n- [x] The dedup key incorporates the material fingerprint (asserted by the tests above).\n- [x] A follow-up ticket for W2 exists, linked `discovered_from` this ticket.\n\n## Testing\n\n```\n.venv/bin/pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_llm_dedup.py tests/unit/test_mcp_inflight.py tests/unit/test_mcp_gate_async.py -p no:randomly -q\nmake lint && make typecheck && make verify\n```\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1792377756775836001,"uuid":"9aa0e499-5b6e-4322-a6df-84ddd4b18d51"}